WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:02.049
- He was an American blues singer,

00:00:02.049 --> 00:00:04.087
song writer, and guitarist.

00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:08.195
He was inducted into the Rock
and Roll Hall of Fame in 1987.

00:00:08.195 --> 00:00:09.419
He's considered one of the most

00:00:09.419 --> 00:00:12.275
influential blues musicians of all time,

00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:14.892
earning the nickname, the king of blues.

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:18.815
He's B.B. King and here are
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:18.815 --> 00:00:20.898
(swoosh)

00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:23.407
- What do you want to do with your music

00:00:23.407 --> 00:00:25.690
and with your singing?

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:27.940
- Play the best that I can,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.974
reach as many people as I can,

00:00:31.974 --> 00:00:32.906
as many countries.

00:00:32.906 --> 00:00:34.071
In other words,

00:00:34.071 --> 00:00:36.215
I'd like the whole
world to be able to hear

00:00:36.215 --> 00:00:38.427
B.B. King sing and play the blues.

00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:42.011
Well, practice is something that we think

00:00:42.011 --> 00:00:43.758
is something that you
should do daily anyway

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:45.562
'cuz I'm lazy and I don't,

00:00:45.562 --> 00:00:49.382
but generally you kind
of think, for example,

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.495
I know that at the North Sea Jazz Festival

00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:56.662
people are used to hearing
various types of music.

00:00:57.972 --> 00:01:00.302
So, when I come out,

00:01:00.302 --> 00:01:04.105
I try to do the best that I
can do of my kind of music.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.587
- B.B., do you still practice?

00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:07.252
- Yes. Yes, I do.

00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:08.664
Not nearly enough,

00:01:08.664 --> 00:01:11.664
but I play every night, practically,

00:01:12.531 --> 00:01:15.750
at least for five nights a week

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.000
and I practice,

00:01:17.981 --> 00:01:19.398
but my practicing

00:01:22.187 --> 00:01:23.708
is not what it should be.

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:24.541
In other words,

00:01:24.541 --> 00:01:27.720
to really just get down and
play the scales and something

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.470
once or twice a week.

00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:33.777
But by me playing every night, usually,

00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:35.266
I'm always running into something

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:38.202
or accidentally falling into something

00:01:38.202 --> 00:01:39.308
that I hadn't heard before,

00:01:39.308 --> 00:01:41.916
then you go back and look for it later on.

00:01:41.916 --> 00:01:42.783
- When you do practice,

00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:43.778
what do you work on?

00:01:43.778 --> 00:01:46.190
Do you have any specific exercises?

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.023
- My exercises usually

00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:52.066
it's the scales mostly.

00:01:52.066 --> 00:01:55.899
(descending two octave scale)

00:01:57.398 --> 00:02:00.446
Mostly that and just all
over the neck of the guitar

00:02:00.446 --> 00:02:02.834
because if I keep doing it,

00:02:02.834 --> 00:02:04.417
I usually will hear

00:02:05.736 --> 00:02:07.793
and you start to playing with them.

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:10.543
(melodic improv)

00:02:15.893 --> 00:02:16.967
You keep hearing it

00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:18.805
and you hear things
that you can play with.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:21.555
(melodic improv)

00:02:25.642 --> 00:02:27.384
You know, something like that.
- That's great.

00:02:27.384 --> 00:02:28.806
- And that's what I keep doing

00:02:28.806 --> 00:02:31.056
until my fingers stay stop.

00:02:32.174 --> 00:02:33.836
- [Man] That feeling on the guitar,

00:02:33.836 --> 00:02:35.503
that singing string.

00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:38.854
What was the phrase that
you used for it before?

00:02:38.854 --> 00:02:40.463
- Twingy.
- Twingy.

00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:41.460
This is your own.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.991
This is your contribution.

00:02:43.991 --> 00:02:45.074
- I think so.

00:02:47.287 --> 00:02:48.944
I think it is

00:02:48.944 --> 00:02:51.248
because I've heard so many guys,

00:02:51.248 --> 00:02:53.425
since I've started
playing, sound that way,

00:02:53.425 --> 00:02:57.075
and I don't remember ever
hearing anybody before that.

00:02:57.075 --> 00:02:58.698
Not before that.

00:02:58.698 --> 00:03:00.531
We average about 200--

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.923
about 250 to 275 concerts per year

00:03:07.356 --> 00:03:11.174
and sometimes that mean you
might do two in one day.

00:03:11.174 --> 00:03:12.427
I'm 69.

00:03:12.427 --> 00:03:16.594
I'll be 70 on September the
16th if I live that long.

00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:21.253
And I find the energy meeting
people like yourselves.

00:03:21.253 --> 00:03:22.503
I enjoy people.

00:03:23.716 --> 00:03:24.966
I love to play.

00:03:25.825 --> 00:03:27.950
See people happy.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:29.819
I love to see them dance

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:32.315
and see them enjoy themselves.

00:03:32.315 --> 00:03:34.061
That's energy for me

00:03:34.061 --> 00:03:35.186
and it's educational.

00:03:35.186 --> 00:03:38.319
I get a chance to learn
how to say merci beaucoup.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:40.169
(laughing)

00:03:40.169 --> 00:03:41.159
I don't speak French,

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:43.148
but Lucille does.

00:03:43.148 --> 00:03:45.635
Music is a universal language

00:03:45.635 --> 00:03:47.769
and I believe that because

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:50.015
it's been many times I've
heard great musicians

00:03:50.015 --> 00:03:51.862
like Django Reinhardt.

00:03:51.862 --> 00:03:53.314
I love.

00:03:53.314 --> 00:03:54.572
You never hear him speak English,

00:03:54.572 --> 00:03:57.505
but he speaks it with his guitar.

00:03:57.505 --> 00:04:00.058
So, I think music is a universal language

00:04:00.058 --> 00:04:04.058
that we can, I believe,
communicate with anyone.

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:06.521
Well--

00:04:08.224 --> 00:04:09.891
Yes. I believe I do.

00:04:11.246 --> 00:04:13.274
I don't want to stick my neck out there,

00:04:13.274 --> 00:04:14.524
but I think so.

00:04:15.362 --> 00:04:19.370
But I'm grateful that some
of them seem to like me.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:21.037
I'm grateful because

00:04:22.214 --> 00:04:25.797
to me, it seemed to
open a few doors for us

00:04:26.736 --> 00:04:31.150
that seemed like it was
never going to be open

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.727
and we're glad when people

00:04:32.727 --> 00:04:34.952
like Mike and the rest of the fellows

00:04:34.952 --> 00:04:37.740
will take up some of the things we do.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.073
We're very happy

00:04:39.978 --> 00:04:43.731
because until the days of rock and roll,

00:04:43.731 --> 00:04:45.597
a lot of times

00:04:45.597 --> 00:04:47.158
a lot of the places,

00:04:47.158 --> 00:04:49.656
they just wouldn't accept us.

00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:51.681
I'm not speaking racially.

00:04:51.681 --> 00:04:53.974
I'm just talking about
he people as a whole

00:04:53.974 --> 00:04:55.981
that just wouldn't accept
us in some of the places

00:04:55.981 --> 00:04:57.736
that the doors are open now,

00:04:57.736 --> 00:04:59.065
that you can go into

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:02.126
because of people like Mike,

00:05:02.126 --> 00:05:05.459
Elvis Presley, the Beatles, Fats Domino.

00:05:06.486 --> 00:05:09.291
People like that helped
us out quite a bit.

00:05:09.291 --> 00:05:11.609
Well, I think, like this--

00:05:11.609 --> 00:05:14.732
the management of Newport
Club wouldn't want me

00:05:14.732 --> 00:05:18.468
if they couldn't have people
that wanted to see me.

00:05:18.468 --> 00:05:21.347
So, I try my best to let the audience know

00:05:21.347 --> 00:05:23.914
that I appreciate them

00:05:23.914 --> 00:05:26.664
and their appearance here with me

00:05:27.543 --> 00:05:30.043
or their support, if you will,

00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:35.626
in letting them know that I love them too.

00:05:35.626 --> 00:05:37.472
I've been around some artists,

00:05:37.472 --> 00:05:40.595
I've been around some places, even.

00:05:40.595 --> 00:05:42.360
Once, the audience--

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.205
I'll never forget once, I was in Chicago.

00:05:44.205 --> 00:05:45.202
I won't mention the club.

00:05:45.202 --> 00:05:46.456
I was in Chicago

00:05:46.456 --> 00:05:49.798
and the management had been advertising

00:05:49.798 --> 00:05:51.743
to get people to come.

00:05:51.743 --> 00:05:52.695
So, people were there.

00:05:52.695 --> 00:05:54.159
So, when the show was over,

00:05:54.159 --> 00:05:55.526
some guy got up and said,

00:05:55.526 --> 00:05:57.292
well, you don't have to go home,

00:05:57.292 --> 00:05:59.336
but you got to leave here.

00:05:59.336 --> 00:06:00.515
That made me angry.

00:06:00.515 --> 00:06:01.528
I get very angry.

00:06:01.528 --> 00:06:02.992
Very angry.

00:06:02.992 --> 00:06:04.230
I said that he could say,

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:06.216
folks, we got to close.

00:06:06.216 --> 00:06:07.405
You know?

00:06:07.405 --> 00:06:09.887
That whole stuff about
you don't have to go home

00:06:09.887 --> 00:06:11.475
but you got to leave here.

00:06:11.475 --> 00:06:12.383
He didn't say that when he was trying

00:06:12.383 --> 00:06:14.327
to get them to come in.

00:06:14.327 --> 00:06:16.872
So, I wasn't happy about that at all.

00:06:16.872 --> 00:06:18.944
So, you don't get that here.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.774
Sure. There are curfews in some places.

00:06:21.774 --> 00:06:23.608
Some places you have to
close at a particular time.

00:06:23.608 --> 00:06:25.782
The audience understands that.

00:06:25.782 --> 00:06:26.934
So, if you tell them,

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:28.496
you're going to have a
few that may grumble,

00:06:28.496 --> 00:06:30.391
but the majority of the people

00:06:30.391 --> 00:06:32.580
are right with you and they're sensible.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.059
They know that if you don't close down,

00:06:34.059 --> 00:06:35.115
you maybe lose your license.

00:06:35.115 --> 00:06:37.046
So, if you say, folks, sorry.

00:06:37.046 --> 00:06:38.367
The time is now.

00:06:38.367 --> 00:06:39.824
We have to cut it off.

00:06:39.824 --> 00:06:41.758
It's our curfew time.

00:06:41.758 --> 00:06:44.125
And majority of the people will walk out.

00:06:44.125 --> 00:06:45.289
They will go.

00:06:45.289 --> 00:06:48.054
- [Man] What is for
you the characteristics

00:06:48.054 --> 00:06:49.471
of a good melody?

00:06:52.152 --> 00:06:55.485
- Something that seems to stay with you.

00:06:56.554 --> 00:06:59.072
Sometimes you wake up mornings

00:06:59.072 --> 00:07:01.405
and you go to wash your face

00:07:02.489 --> 00:07:06.341
and you still hear there's
something in your head.

00:07:06.341 --> 00:07:07.989
You walk out

00:07:07.989 --> 00:07:10.847
and all of a sudden you're whistling.

00:07:10.847 --> 00:07:12.958
(whistling)

00:07:12.958 --> 00:07:16.042
Something that stays with you.

00:07:16.042 --> 00:07:17.542
That's a good melody.

00:07:17.542 --> 00:07:18.705
That haunts you.

00:07:18.705 --> 00:07:21.278
- [Man] Are you still getting
better as a guitarist?

00:07:21.278 --> 00:07:23.259
- I hope I am.

00:07:23.259 --> 00:07:24.734
I hope so.

00:07:24.734 --> 00:07:27.909
I can't always tell
'cuz I still can't play

00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:29.825
like I want to play.

00:07:29.825 --> 00:07:31.725
I'm going to do this 'til I die, folks.

00:07:31.725 --> 00:07:34.225
(blues music)

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.460
There's a lot of young guitarists
have new ways of playing

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:42.043
and they, to me, are
what's going on today.

00:07:44.261 --> 00:07:46.217
- Well, people want to see you.

00:07:46.217 --> 00:07:47.050
- I'm glad.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.760
(laughing)

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:50.927
Oh, am I glad.

00:07:52.022 --> 00:07:54.522
(blues music)

00:08:06.871 --> 00:08:10.038
So, I was going to Montgomery to play.

00:08:10.978 --> 00:08:12.195
Called the promoter,

00:08:12.195 --> 00:08:14.090
I said, I'm broke down.

00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:17.264
I said, I don't know how long
it's going to take to fix it.

00:08:17.264 --> 00:08:20.247
It was about two thirty, three
o'clock in the evening, then.

00:08:20.247 --> 00:08:23.220
I said, I'm pretty sure that

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.542
we'll be late when we get there.

00:08:25.542 --> 00:08:26.780
He said, that's all right, B.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.345
Come on up.

00:08:28.345 --> 00:08:29.341
Then, are you sure?

00:08:29.341 --> 00:08:31.991
He said, yeah. Come on up. Come on up.

00:08:31.991 --> 00:08:34.654
So, sure enough, we were
late when the bus got ready

00:08:34.654 --> 00:08:37.022
and we went on up there.

00:08:37.022 --> 00:08:39.003
When we got there--

00:08:39.003 --> 00:08:41.253
We had something you call--

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.510
You don't hear much about them today.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.173
Armories.

00:08:47.173 --> 00:08:48.374
Armories. You remember?
- Yeah.

00:08:48.374 --> 00:08:49.467
- We used to play a lot of those.

00:08:49.467 --> 00:08:50.811
Armories.

00:08:50.811 --> 00:08:52.061
So, the armory,

00:08:53.089 --> 00:08:55.105
you started from the ground floor

00:08:55.105 --> 00:08:58.783
and you had to walk about 10 or 15 stairs

00:08:58.783 --> 00:09:01.318
and there was a platform like that.

00:09:01.318 --> 00:09:02.895
So, that's where he was sat

00:09:02.895 --> 00:09:05.311
to collect his tickets and the money.

00:09:05.311 --> 00:09:08.238
And then, you go about
10 or 15 more floors

00:09:08.238 --> 00:09:10.485
and there's the dance hall.

00:09:10.485 --> 00:09:11.318
- Okay.

00:09:11.318 --> 00:09:12.921
- So, when I got there I said,

00:09:12.921 --> 00:09:14.498
we'll, we made it.

00:09:14.498 --> 00:09:16.413
He's, yeah, but you can
turn around and go back now

00:09:16.413 --> 00:09:18.694
'cuz I ain't going to pay you.

00:09:18.694 --> 00:09:21.332
I said, but I called you and told you.

00:09:21.332 --> 00:09:22.483
He said, yeah, but I didn't think

00:09:22.483 --> 00:09:23.980
you were going to be this late.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:24.915
(laughing)

00:09:24.915 --> 00:09:26.729
I said, but I didn't say how late.

00:09:26.729 --> 00:09:29.460
I just told you we're going to be late.

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.626
He said, well. I ain't going to pay you,

00:09:31.626 --> 00:09:32.891
so you can turn around and go back.

00:09:32.891 --> 00:09:35.120
I said, well. Since I'm here,

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.787
I have a thing about being late.

00:09:40.266 --> 00:09:43.933
I have a thing about
standing up the people.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.384
I said, let me go on out and play.

00:09:46.384 --> 00:09:47.603
He said, okay. You can
go up if you want to,

00:09:47.603 --> 00:09:48.779
but I ain't going to pay you.

00:09:48.779 --> 00:09:49.823
I said, okay.

00:09:49.823 --> 00:09:52.133
So, we went on upstairs

00:09:52.133 --> 00:09:54.000
and boy, when we got on the dance floor

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.699
and people just looked like
they were packed in there.

00:09:58.699 --> 00:10:01.489
I sure would have liked to
have had some of that money.

00:10:01.489 --> 00:10:04.133
(laughing)

00:10:04.133 --> 00:10:06.122
So, we got up there

00:10:06.122 --> 00:10:07.585
and we started to playing

00:10:07.585 --> 00:10:09.787
and the people, you know,

00:10:09.787 --> 00:10:11.117
you couldn't buy liquor,

00:10:11.117 --> 00:10:12.308
but you could carry

00:10:12.308 --> 00:10:13.846
what they call a brown bag.

00:10:13.846 --> 00:10:14.679
- Yeah.

00:10:14.679 --> 00:10:16.237
- You could carry your bottle with you.

00:10:16.237 --> 00:10:17.556
Well, at the armory,

00:10:17.556 --> 00:10:21.090
they had places where you could
keep your bottle, you know?

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:22.267
Well, we got to playing

00:10:22.267 --> 00:10:24.680
and people starting
drinking their yucky duck,

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:27.248
everything was going good.

00:10:27.248 --> 00:10:30.683
So, after I played about two
or three pieces, you know,

00:10:30.683 --> 00:10:33.489
and everybody was fired up.

00:10:33.489 --> 00:10:35.969
I said, ladies and gentlemen.

00:10:35.969 --> 00:10:37.892
So you know, this is 'ol B

00:10:37.892 --> 00:10:39.425
and I never stood you up.

00:10:39.425 --> 00:10:41.550
No, B. You always come in time.

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:42.907
I said, yeah.

00:10:42.907 --> 00:10:45.226
Well, I got something sad to tell you,

00:10:45.226 --> 00:10:47.115
but when I came upstairs,

00:10:47.115 --> 00:10:50.958
the boss told me he
wasn't going to pay me.

00:10:50.958 --> 00:10:52.788
Everybody got quiet. What?

00:10:52.788 --> 00:10:55.350
I said, no. He said he
wasn't going to pay me

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:58.203
and I ain't telling you what to do,

00:10:58.203 --> 00:11:01.453
but I feel that if I'm going to play free,

00:11:02.347 --> 00:11:04.897
you should be able to listen free.

00:11:04.897 --> 00:11:06.573
(laughing)

00:11:06.573 --> 00:11:08.187
I ain't going to tell you what to do.

00:11:08.187 --> 00:11:10.177
(laughing)

00:11:10.177 --> 00:11:12.295
They went, he never said
he ain't going to pay me

00:11:12.295 --> 00:11:15.099
and I'm up here playing,

00:11:15.099 --> 00:11:16.911
you ought to check on your money.

00:11:16.911 --> 00:11:18.321
(laughing)

00:11:18.321 --> 00:11:19.427
I ain't telling you to do it,

00:11:19.427 --> 00:11:20.680
but you should.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.405
Boy. A little bit later,

00:11:22.405 --> 00:11:26.325
the money went one way and
the dude went the other.

00:11:26.325 --> 00:11:27.986
And I thought, well,

00:11:27.986 --> 00:11:29.674
the house if full again.

00:11:29.674 --> 00:11:31.047
(laughing)

00:11:31.047 --> 00:11:32.504
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:32.504 --> 00:11:35.839
I made this video because
Arun Kumar asked me to.

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:37.415
So, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:37.415 --> 00:11:39.532
that you want me to profile next,

00:11:39.532 --> 00:11:40.882
leave it in the comments below

00:11:40.882 --> 00:11:42.459
and I will see what I can do.

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:45.242
I'd also love to know which
of B.B. King's top ten rules

00:11:45.242 --> 00:11:47.165
had the greatest impact on you.

00:11:47.165 --> 00:11:49.703
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join in on the discussion.

00:11:49.703 --> 00:11:50.910
Thank you so much for watching.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:54.032
Continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

00:11:54.032 --> 00:11:56.115
(swoosh)

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.660
- Well, everybody that looks at me now.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:06.522
Usually, when I'm talking about my guitar,

00:12:06.522 --> 00:12:08.327
it's always Lucille.

00:12:08.327 --> 00:12:10.827
And they look at me like that.

00:12:11.953 --> 00:12:15.068
And then, I tell them how I gave

00:12:15.068 --> 00:12:18.166
the name Lucille to my guitar.

00:12:18.166 --> 00:12:20.009
We were playing out near a place

00:12:20.009 --> 00:12:21.926
called Twist, Arkansas.

00:12:22.796 --> 00:12:23.760
- [Man] Twist, Arkansas?

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.127
Okay.

00:12:25.127 --> 00:12:28.460
- And they had a place for you to dance,

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:31.569
like over here,

00:12:31.569 --> 00:12:33.540
and you would eat over here

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.483
and that's where I stayed at most times

00:12:35.483 --> 00:12:37.081
when I wasn't on stage.

00:12:37.081 --> 00:12:39.682
They had a small stage, you know,

00:12:39.682 --> 00:12:41.182
smaller than this.

00:12:43.202 --> 00:12:47.696
And I would go in and I
would start to play and eat.

00:12:47.696 --> 00:12:48.950
Eat and play.

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:51.356
(laughing)

00:12:51.356 --> 00:12:52.480
- Sounds good to me.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.230
- And two guys start to fighting.

00:12:57.297 --> 00:12:59.994
They do quite a bit of
that in Mississippi,

00:12:59.994 --> 00:13:01.994
Louisiana, and Arkansas,

00:13:04.096 --> 00:13:08.013
near them small joints
where they gambled then.

00:13:09.047 --> 00:13:10.759
And when these guys started to playing,

00:13:10.759 --> 00:13:12.078
this is in winter,

00:13:12.078 --> 00:13:17.074
they had a big pail sitting
in the middle of the floor

00:13:17.074 --> 00:13:20.798
and one of them knocked the
other one over on this container

00:13:20.798 --> 00:13:22.631
and when he did, well,

00:13:23.807 --> 00:13:25.351
it spilled on the floor.

00:13:25.351 --> 00:13:26.810
And when it spilled on the floor,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.953
everybody started running
out, including me,

00:13:28.953 --> 00:13:31.073
and I forgot my guitar.

00:13:31.073 --> 00:13:33.404
And I'd never done it before

00:13:33.404 --> 00:13:36.501
and never have did it since then.

00:13:36.501 --> 00:13:39.584
But, I went back in and got my guitar

00:13:41.535 --> 00:13:45.552
and I almost lost my
life trying to save it.

00:13:45.552 --> 00:13:48.277
And that's when I named my guitar Lucille

00:13:48.277 --> 00:13:49.940
because these two guys, I learned,

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:52.023
was fighting about a lady

00:13:53.191 --> 00:13:57.879
that worked in the place
where they cooked the food.

00:13:57.879 --> 00:13:59.337
And I named my guitar Lucille

00:13:59.337 --> 00:14:01.232
to remind me to never do that again.

00:14:01.232 --> 00:14:03.250
(laughing)

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:04.532
That's how that came about.

00:14:04.532 --> 00:14:05.813
- You almost lost your life going back in

00:14:05.813 --> 00:14:07.740
to get your guitar.
- My guitar, yeah.

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:08.930
- And you named it Lucille

00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:10.502
and that's stuck all of these years.

00:14:10.502 --> 00:14:11.756
- Yes, all these years.

00:14:11.756 --> 00:14:14.923
(mellow blues music)

